(I don't like/believe in hard rules, but saying "guidelines" sounds confusing and misleading, so...)
* KISS! (Keep it simple)
* * no useless things. no things i don´t need.
* tabs
* case sensitive
* organize assets by FEATURE. NOT by type.
* DO NOT use hungarian notation 
* NO DEPENDENCY INJECTION! (except if approved by jeronimo)
* NO COROUTINES. Only async-tasks and timers.
* NO ASSERTS OR CHECKS. use an actual if and manage the situation.
* NO abstract classes. base classes have to be instance-ables. The methods can be emptied but the instance needs to not break (crash). Better to have a base functionality there.
* * No Final either
* NO INTERFACES
** make sensible base classes.
* UI defines base class on cpp to interface with.
** cpp controls ui via virtual functions
** ui communicates back with delegates. ui doesn't communicate directly with other stuff. except for setting things on subsystems.
* Minimal usage of lambdas, and only if less than 10 LOC. otherwise make a function.
* Use BlueprintNativeEvent when interacting with blueprints and ui. Never use BlueprintImplementableEvent. NEVER EVER use delegates for this case.
** Use BlueprintNativeEvents EVEN if the cpp body is empty. keep the control in cpp. avoid having function chains that only exists on bp.
* Use minimal decoupling. Use polymorphism to achieve it.
** This is tricky if you're not me (hard to explain). Check the code for examples, and ask me to verify.
** Avoid usage of events. no event driven anything.
** Ensure a direct top-down hierarchy.
*** parents call children directly through functions.
*** child communicate with parents through delegates.
** Do use subsystems
** Do use delegates
*** But avoid having stuff self-registering automatically. Tell them to initialize instead. 
** Do not use asynchronous nodes (either mine or from engine when possible)
* forward declare as much as possible. includes on cpp instead of .h
* avoid overusing set/gets. public vars are fine when makes sense.
** flexibility is more important than "university purity".
* Ensure proper lifecycle. Initialize variables. And nullify/destroy on endplay or when needed.
* USE DEFAULT VARIABLES VALUES, (instead of) initializing variables in custom constructors. except variables that will be overwritten.
** NEVER initialize using the member initialization list (on the constructor)
** ok to initialize _inside_ the constructor (with regular assignment) only if the value is non-default, and specific to that constructor.
* Use UPROPERTY on all pointers. nullify at end if is transient.
** Use Transient on all variables that are transient.
** NEVER use Edit* on components as it can break serialization. Also add VisiblyAnywhere for most.
** ALWAYS use Transient for delegates. don't bind on constructor.
* use const& widely. 
** SPECIALLY on delegates and for loops
** To Pass structures as you WILL modify them in the body.
** same for variables in functions.
* pure functions must be simple and not contain complex calculations. pure are very inefficient they get called multiple times. force bp to have a local var or execution pin. it might be only for loops (test this out) but in general is a way to say 'be careful when calling this, its precious'
* use MoveTemp when possible.
* DO NOT define structures or types on BP only. create stubs in cpp then inherit. this is contagious.
* Don't be overly private. 
** Make your functions be public (when calling them from outside won't break). Aim to make your functions able to be public.
** Make your functions and variable accessible from bps. and callable.
* Avoid using interfaces. use inheritance whenever possible.
* callbacks are delegates
* use subsystems instead of singletons
* * unless you need a replicated actor. then use manager actors. and try using AInfo.
* use TObjectPtr for UPROPERTY member attributes and collections.
* * Potentially other uses that exceeds the scope of the function where its used.
* * https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/unreal-engine-5-migration-guide
* Spawn objects in GameMode::Init instead of having them on the editor when possible
* * Objects that don't belong to a specific datalayer.
* Use TSoftObjectPtr when referencing actors in the world
* * above it fixes the issue of an actor referencing another actor in a different data layer.
* * This way you can reference actors in code.
* * Don't load them unnecessarily.
Metas:
objects
* use readonly if i don't want the pointer to change (eg when instantiated on cpp) (eg create default subobject)
** use visibleDefaultsOnly to allow to edit on the editor 
* use readwrite if i want to change the object
** use Edit* if i want to be able to re-assign the object (with readwrite)

delegates
* make transient to avoid issues when binding on constructor

weird stuff:
* BlueprintNativeEvents do not work on constructors.
* BlueprintNativeEvents cant pass a "UObject* CONST var" as parameter. only UObject* var
